{#
mail/_frame_document.html.twig
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The document a message body is shown in: the sandboxed frame's whole page.

Its own file because two things build it and they must build the same bytes.
_message_body.html.twig fills it with the server's sanitized body. For an
encrypted message the server has no body, so it renders this once with the
placeholder `<!--plmail:body-->` and hands it to the browser, which puts the
body it decrypted where the placeholder is (assets/crypto/message.js). The
frame script is allowed by the hash of its exact text (MessageFrameScript), so
a copy kept in JavaScript would stop running the day either one changed.

Variables:
  render  MessageRender
  body    string, already sanitized
-#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="{{ render.csp }}">
{# Every link in a mail body opens away from the app. MailBodySanitizer already
   forces target="_blank" per link; this covers anything it did not author. #}
<base target="_blank">
<style>
    /* The mail sheet, carried across the document boundary.
       CSS custom properties do not inherit into a frame, and an opaque-origin
       frame cannot be reached from outside to be styled — so these are the
       fallbacks that paint immediately, and the parent controller posts the
       live theme values over the top as soon as it can read them. Every theme's
       sheet is light (see app.css), so a light default is never far wrong and
       there is no flash of the wrong colours. */
    :root { color-scheme: light; }
    html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; }
    body {
        background: #fff;
        color: #27272a;
        font: 14px/1.55 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
        word-break: break-word;
        overflow-x: auto;
    }
    a { color: #1d4ed8; }
    /* Senders assume a viewport. Without this a 700px table adds a horizontal
       scrollbar to a pane that used to just show it. */
    img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
    /* A blocked image keeps its box so the layout holds, and says so. */
    img[data-plmail-blocked] {
        min-width: 12px; min-height: 12px;
        background: repeating-linear-gradient(135deg, #f4f4f5 0 6px, #e4e4e7 6px 12px);
        outline: 1px dashed #a1a1aa;
        outline-offset: -1px;
    }
    /* Two ceilings, because a placeholder's job is to say an image was here,
       not to reserve every pixel of one.

       The generous one applies to a box we shaped from the sender's own
       numbers: those are honest, and the only thing wrong with an honest
       1200px box is that it is 1200px of hatching. It can grow to a screenful
       and no further. The cost is a jump when such an image is un-blocked,
       paid only by images already too tall to take in at once.

       The tight one is for an image whose shape nobody declared. `height:auto`
       needs an intrinsic ratio to compute from and the blank placeholder's is
       1:1, so a width and no height makes a square — 600px of hatching for a
       banner. There is nothing to be faithful to there, so it stays small. */
    img[data-plmail-blocked] { max-height: 24rem; }
    img[data-plmail-blocked]:not([data-plmail-box]) { max-height: 8rem; }
</style>
</head><body>
{{ body|raw }}
<script>{{ render.frameScript|raw }}</script>
</body></html>